<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="chain-rule-complete">
    <title>Directional Derivative, Total Derivative, and the Chain Rule (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen how we can use the calculus 1 derivative to define partial derivatives for multivariable
                        functions. We'll see how we can use partial derivatives to establish more tools, all with the 
                        purpose of of understanding the rate of change of a multivariable function.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Directional Derivatives</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       For <m>f(x,y)</m>, <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> helped us describe the rate of change of <m>f</m>
                       in the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> directions, respectively. But in the <m>x-y</m> plane, there are lots more
                       directions!
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        To specify a direction, we can use a vector of magnitude <m>1</m>, called a <term>unit vector</term>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For a unit vector <m>\uvec = \langle a, b\rangle </m>, the rate of change of <m>f(x,y)</m> in the direction
                        <m>\uvec</m> at the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> is given by 
                        <md>
                            <mrow>D_{\uvec}f(x_0,y_0) \amp= \langle f_x(x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0) \rangle \cdot  \langle a, b\rangle</mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= af_x + bf_y </mrow>
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The vector <m>\langle f_x(x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0) \rangle</m> is called the <term>gradient</term> of <m>f</m>
                        at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>, and is commonly denoted as <m>\nabla f</m>. This vector points in the direction
                        of steepest ascent of <m>f</m> at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Example: Directional Derivative</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Consider <m>f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</m> near the point <m>(1,2)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We can compute <m>\nabla f(x,y) = \langle 2x, -2y \rangle</m>, so that <m>\nabla f(1,2) = \langle 2,-4 \rangle</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If we'd like to know how <m>f</m> changes in the direction <m>\langle -1, 1 \rangle </m>, we first need the 
                        associated unit vector, <m>\uvec = \langle \frac{-1}{\sqrt{2}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m>. Then we compute
                        <md>
                            <mrow>D_{\uvec}f  \amp= \nabla f \cdot \uvec </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \frac{-2}{\sqrt{2}} - \frac{4}{\sqrt{2}} </mrow>
                        </md>
                        Since this is less than zero, we know the function decreases in the direction <m>\uvec</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We could also compute in which direction(s) <m>\vvec = \langle v_1, v_2 \rangle</m> the function stays constant. This is solving <m>D_{\vvec}f = 0</m>, which 
                        is equivalent to <m>2v_1 - 4v_2 = 0</m>, which means <m>v_1 = 2v_2</m>. If we require that <m>\vvec</m> is a unit vector,
                        then we obtain <m>v_1 = \pm \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}</m> and <m>v_2 = \pm \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        See <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/cp4zwda5ob"> the directional derivative Desmos interactive</url> to visualize
                        all of these computations.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Total Differential</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Partial derivatives let one variable change, and hold all others constant, which is not always 
                        desirable in practice (we'd like multiple variables to change at the same time). Directional derivatives
                        are one tool, as they allow us to <q>walk</q> in any direction and see how a function changes. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Another tool is the <term>total derivative</term>, which makes use of a surface's tangent plane:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>P(T,V)</m> gives the pressure of a gas at temperature <m>T</m> and volume <m>V</m>. How 
                        does <m>P</m> change as both <m>T</m> and <m>V</m> change?
                    </p>
                </statement>
              <solution>
                <p>
                    Using the equation of a tangent plane, we can write that for a temperature/volume pair <m>(T,V)</m> close 
                    to the initial temperature/volume pair <m>(T_0, V_0)</m>, 
                    <md>
                        P(T,V) \approx P(T_0,V_0) + \frac{\partial P}{\partial T} (T-T_0) +  \frac{\partial P}{\partial V} (V-V_0)
                    </md>,
                    
                    which means 
                    <md>
                        \Delta P \approx \frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \Delta T +  \frac{\partial P}{\partial V} \Delta V
                    </md>.

                    The <term>total differential</term> is the approximate value we get from using the tangent plane on the right hand side, written
                    <md>
                        dP =\frac{\partial P}{\partial T} dT +  \frac{\partial P}{\partial V} dV
                    </md>.

                    When <m>\Delta T</m> and <m>\Delta V</m> are small, the true change in <m>P</m> is very close to the total differential; that is,
                    <md>
                        \Delta P \approx dP
                    </md>.
                    
                    
                    
                    
                </p>
              </solution>
                
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Total Derivative</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        It is common that variables in our function change with respect to a single common variable (like time):
                        <md>
                            P(T,V) = P(T(t), V(t))
                        </md>,
                        which is a composition of functions.
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In this case, <m>dP = \frac{\partial P}{\partial T} dT + \frac{\partial P}{\partial V} dV</m> becomes
                    </p>
                </statement>
              <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \frac{dP}{dt} = \frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \frac{dT}{dt} + \frac{\partial P}{\partial V} \frac{dV}{dt}
                    </md>,
                    which is called the <term>total derivative</term>. This gives the approximate change in <m>P</m> for a small change 
                    in <m>t</m> at a fixed temperature and volume.
                    
                </p>
              </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>

<page>
            <exercise workspace="5in">
            <title>Example: Total Differential and Total Derivative</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Let <m>P(T,V) = \frac{T}{V}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The total differential of <m>P</m> is
                        <md>
                            dP = \frac{1}{V}dT - \frac{T}{V^2}dV
                        </md>.

                        If <m>V=10</m> L and <m>T=273</m> K, let's compute the approximate change in pressure if the volume 
                        decreases to <m>9</m> L and temperature increases to <m>274</m> K. Using the total differential, we compute
                        <md>
                            <mrow>dP \amp= \frac{1}{10} \cdot 1 - \frac{273}{100} \cdot (-1) </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= 0.1  + 2.73 </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= 2.83 </mrow>
                        </md>
                        Note this is an approximation of <m>\Delta P</m>, which in this example we can compute exactly as 
                        <m>\Delta P = \frac{274}{9} - \frac{273}{10} \approx 3.14</m>.
                        
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Now suppose <m>T(t) = \sin(t)</m> and <m>V(t) = t^2</m>. Then we can compute the total derivative as 
                        <md>
                             <mrow>\frac{dP}{dt} \amp= \frac{1}{V}\cos(t) - \frac{T}{V^2}(2t) </mrow>
                             <mrow> \amp= \frac{\cos(t)}{t^2} - \frac{2t\sin(t)}{t^4} </mrow>
                        </md>
                        We could verify this derivative directly by writing <m>P(T,V)</m> as a function of the single variable 
                        <m>t</m>, and using derivative rules from calculus 1.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

           
</page>

<page>
     <exercise workspace="3in">
            <title>The Chain Rule</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                   The total derivative is an example of the <term>multivariable chain rule</term>. In general, tree diagrams
                   can be very helpful for keeping track of this rule in more complicated variables. 
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x(t),y(t))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for a tree diagram. It should give the formula
                        <md>
                            \frac{df}{dt} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x(t),y(t), z(t))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                 <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for a tree diagram. It should give the formula
                        <md>
                            \frac{df}{dt} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial z} \frac{\partial z}{\partial t}
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x(s,t),y(s,t))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See notes from class for a tree diagram. It should give the formulas
                        <md>
                            \frac{\partial f}{\partial s} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial s} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial s} 
                        </md>
                        and
                        <md>
                            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} 
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
                
            </exercise>
</page>

   

       
    </worksheet>
